        "title": "Shear Strength: Mohr-Coulomb, Triaxial Tests, Critical State, Residual Strength",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Soil fails in shear — not compression or tension. When you push soil to its limit (under a footing, inside a slope, behind a retaining wall), it mobilizes shearing resistance along a failure surface. Understanding what governs that resistance — and how it changes with drainage conditions and stress history — is the core of geotechnical strength analysis."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-SHEAR-STRENGTH.md#003",
      "path": [
        "04-SHEAR-STRENGTH.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:geotechnical-engineering:04-shear-strength, git-history:geotechnical-engineering:04-shear-strength]",
        "canonical_path": "geotechnical-engineering/04-SHEAR-STRENGTH.md",
        "concepts": "[shear, strength]",
        "current_path": "geotechnical-engineering/04-SHEAR-STRENGTH.md",
        "id": "maxim:geotechnical-engineering:shear-strength",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "geotechnical-engineering",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[shear, strength]",
        "section": "geotechnical-engineering",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Shear Strength: Mohr-Coulomb, Triaxial Tests, Critical State, Residual Strength",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|               SHEAR STRENGTH — CONCEPTUAL MAP                    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  MOHR-COULOMB FAILURE CRITERION                                  |\n|  τ_f = c' + σ'_n × tan(φ')     [effective stress parameters]   |\n|  τ_f = cu (= Su)               [total stress for undrained]     |\n|                                                                  |\n|  KEY DISTINCTION:                                                |\n|  DRAINED analysis: stresses change slowly; pore pressure        |\n|  ≈ hydrostatic throughout loading; use c', φ'                   |\n|                                                                  |\n|  UNDRAINED analysis: stresses change faster than drainage;      |\n|  excess pore pressure develops; use Su = cu (φ=0 analysis)      |\n|                                                                  |\n|  WHEN DOES EACH APPLY?                                           |\n|  Sand: always drained (high k → pore pressure dissipates fast)  |\n|  Soft clay: short-term = undrained; long-term = drained         |\n|  Stiff clay: often drained controls (swells long-term)          |\n|                                                                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
